WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.290
- [Narrator] The Federal Reserve

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.720
keeps raising interest
rates to fight inflation.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.030
Today, it's at nearly 4%.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:07.410
- And we continue to anticipate

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.800
that ongoing increases
will be appropriate.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.180
- [Narrator] But what
about the interest rates

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.450
that make you money like
on your savings account?

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.820
Right now, the average
savings account pays

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.280
less than a quarter of a percent

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.380
but some savings accounts are going

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.850
up just as fast as the Fed's.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.530
- You know a lot of what you experience

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:26.820
with what's happened with savings rates

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.620
really depends on where you're looking.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.900
- [Narrator] Here's why most
savings accounts are lagging

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.843
behind the Fed's rising
rates and which ones aren't.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.330
Let's start with why a bank

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.550
pays you interest in the first place.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.100
It's because, well, they need your money.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.290
Banks use the deposits
people keep with them

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.530
to fund loans like for
cars or credit cards

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.990
and they profit off of that interest.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:52.440
Basically, they need these
deposits in order to make money,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.680
which is why they entice
you with savings rates.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.900
- The savings rate that a
particular bank is paying

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.950
is a reflection of their
desire for additional deposits

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.190
or their attempt to retain
the deposits that they have.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.270
That's it.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:07.860
- [Narrator] Greg
McBride works at Bankrate

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.830
which compiles this data
on savings accounts.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.810
- We survey financial institutions

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.490
for rate and fee information

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.370
on a wide range of
consumer banking products

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:18.990
- [Narrator] And there is a wide range

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.480
all in competition to keep your money.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:22.950
A traditional savings account

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.070
with your regular bank
isn't the only safe place.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.070
There's also online savings
accounts, which traditionally,

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.340
pay more interest than your
local brick and mortar bank.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.340
Then there's CDs or
certificates of deposits.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.470
Basically, the bank will
pay more in interest,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.600
but only if you keep that money with them

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.370
for a set amount of time.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.860
It's similar to a
government treasury bond.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.590
You earn more interest,
but your money is tied up

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.070
not readily accessible like
these savings accounts.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:53.640
But with all these choices,
if the rate rises on one,

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:57.360
it usually pressures the
others to rise too, usually.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.820
- If banks want to retain deposits,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.170
they've gotta keep pace with

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.300
what government bonds are yielding.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.280
Otherwise, savers will take their money

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.620
out of the bank and put
it in government bonds.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.670
- [Narrator] Those government bonds

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.920
are yielding high Interest Rates though.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.620
They are keeping up with
the fed's rising rates.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.110
So, why aren't these savings accounts?

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.790
The answer lies in this chart.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:21.870
The amount of money currently
held as deposits at banks.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.870
You can see this huge spike here in 2020,

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.160
where people stopped spending,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.220
started saving and
received stimulus checks.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.240
The banks were flush with
deposits and they still are.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.700
Experts like Greg say
not only do big banks

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.530
not need to attract deposits,

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.440
but also see rising
rates as an opportunity.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.200
- Well interest Rates were
at record lows for years.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.160
And so, what happened was

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.980
that squeezed bank interest margins.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.780
- [Narrator] Banks don't
wanna pay more in interest

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.580
than they're bringing in, right.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.100
For a long time, even before the pandemic,

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.050
the Feds kept interest rates low

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.700
meaning the rate they profited from

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.980
like car loans was also low.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.900
Now that the fed's rate is going up,

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.700
meaning these rates are too,

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.100
they're not going to
increase the savings rate

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.020
unless they really have to.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.320
And overall banks have so
many deposits right now,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.340
they don't.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.260
- A lot of the bigger banks in particular

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.917
they're sitting on a mountain of deposits.

00:03:14.917 --> 00:03:17.160
They have more deposits than
they know what to do with.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.835
So the last thing they're gonna do

00:03:18.835 --> 00:03:21.570
is raise cost of that.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.510
- [Narrator] So who is
paying a competitive rate?

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:25.500
Online accounts,

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.660
often called high yield savings accounts

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:31.890
are offering savings
rates as high as 3.5%.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.110
- Banks that are eager for deposits,

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.270
that have a growing loan operation

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:40.050
and need to feed that machine
by bringing in more deposits,

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:43.320
they will pay increasingly
competitive rates.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.430
- These types of accounts

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.310
are still FDIC protected like any bank.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.460
You just have to transfer money
in and out of them online,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:51.990
which could take a few days

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:53.520
as opposed to being able to make

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.410
a same day withdrawal in person.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.760
- If you're not taking any risk,

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.760
would you rather earn 3 1/2%
or would you rather earn 0.01%?

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.590
I mean, that's a slam dunk. That's easy.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.420
- [Narrator] So why isn't
your savings account

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.580
going up with the Fed's rising rates?

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.006
It could be because you have
your money in the wrong place.

00:04:12.006 --> 00:04:14.756
(cheerful music)

